Here’s a tight, copy-pasteable way to fit tail-aware copulas with pyvinecopulib (Python) that actually capture crash/rally co-moves.

⸻

0) Setup & inputs
	•	Input data: your standardized residuals (after AR–GARCH), shape (T, d) for d=5 (PC1–PC4, ΔlogS).
	•	Don’t Cholesky-whiten before a copula; use ranks (pseudo-observations) so margins can be anything.

import numpy as np, pyvinecopulib as pv
from scipy import stats

# Z: T x d standardized residuals, after any residualization (e.g., PCs ~ ΔlogS)
def pseudo_obs(col):
    r = stats.rankdata(col, method="average")
    return r / (len(col) + 1.0)

def to_pobs(Z):
    return np.column_stack([pseudo_obs(Z[:,j]) for j in range(Z.shape[1])])

U = to_pobs(Z)   # U in (0,1)^d


⸻

1) Fit a tail-aware R-vine

Let the algorithm pick the best pair-copula per edge, including t (symmetric tails) and Clayton/Gumbel (asymmetric tails) with rotations (to capture negative/upper/lower tails).

controls = pv.FitControlsVinecop(
    family_set=[
        pv.BicopFamily.student,   # symmetric tail dep
        pv.BicopFamily.clayton,   # lower-tail dep
        pv.BicopFamily.gumbel,    # upper-tail dep
        pv.BicopFamily.gaussian,  # no tail dep
        pv.BicopFamily.frank      # no tail dep (but flexible center)
    ],
    rotations=True,                   # allow 90/180/270° (handles signs/asymmetry)
    selection_criterion=pv.FitMethod.aic,
    parametric_method=pv.ParametricMethod.mle
)

vine = pv.Vinecop(U, controls)  # structure + families + params are selected
print(vine)                     # shows tree structure and chosen families

Tip: If you want a simpler global model first, restrict family_set=[pv.BicopFamily.student, pv.BicopFamily.gaussian] to compare “t vs Gaussian only.”

⸻

2) Quick tail-fit diagnostics (does it capture crashes?)

Compute empirical vs model co-exceedances in tails; this is the most practical check.

def tail_stats(U, model, pairs=None, q_list=(0.05, 0.02, 0.01), nsim=200_000, seed=7):
    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)
    if pairs is None:
        d = U.shape[1]
        pairs = [(i,j) for i in range(d) for j in range(i+1,d)]
    Usim = model.simulate(nsim, seeds=[int(rng.integers(1e9))])
    out = []
    for (i,j) in pairs:
        for q in q_list:
            empL = np.mean((U[:,i] < q) & (U[:,j] < q))
            empU = np.mean((U[:,i] > 1-q) & (U[:,j] > 1-q))
            modL = np.mean((Usim[:,i] < q) & (Usim[:,j] < q))
            modU = np.mean((Usim[:,i] > 1-q) & (Usim[:,j] > 1-q))
            out.append(((i,j), q, empL, modL, empU, modU))
    return out

stats_tbl = tail_stats(U, vine)
for (i,j), q, empL, modL, empU, modU in stats_tbl:
    print(f"pair {i}-{j} | q={q:.2%} | lower emp={empL:.4f} vs model={modL:.4f} | upper emp={empU:.4f} vs model={modU:.4f}")

	•	If model lower-tail probs are too small vs empirical for pairs with ΔlogS → allow Clayton (and rotated Clayton for negative dependence) or push toward t with lower ν by narrowing family_set.
	•	If upper tails are heavier → ensure Gumbel is allowed.

